\f1\fs22 \'91You're doing that thing again,\'92 Sarah told Hayley, as she slid into the seat across from her. Sarah shivered. Spring came slowly to this part of the country, and the fresh air did nothing to retain the heat. Though the balcony windows of her bedroom drew the morning sun and waked her as gently as toasting a crumpet, the kitchen faced west and, at this time of morning, the townhouse\'92s shadow cast almost as far as the back fence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What thing?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah patted her belly beneath her Shakti Enterprises lab coat, glad to put her attentions anywhere but on her medical appointment with Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That thing where you stare through your coffee like it contains the secrets of the universe.\'92 Sarah gulped down her own potential secrets of the universe and hoped Hayley would be an engaging diversion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley forced herself to focus on her friend's face. \'91I need to access the project archives,\'92 she said abruptly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which project?\'92 Sarah felt Little Clinton kick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91All of them. Everything Shakti Enterprises has researched that\'92s Gyges Disc adjacent.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91As far as I know, Project Kebab is the first one.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley stared at Sarah like she\'92d been skipping school. \'91Would you like to guess how many times I\'92ve heard the phrase, 
\f2\i This is the first time I\'92ve ever done something like this officer, I swear
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you calling Eloise a criminal?\'92 Sarah asked, just for sport.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Warlord, criminal. If the cuffs fit\'85\'92 Hayley shrugged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the first time, Sarah considered whether Hayley was just like any other cop, looking to make things \'93fit\'94. It jolted her for a moment, the decided to wade past it. \'91I doubt Eloise would want any of us just fossicking around in her proprietary work product.\'92 Saying it like that made Sarah want to fossick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m not asking Eloise.\'92 The words emerged harder than Hayley intended. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you asking for my blessing?\'92 Sarah needed to sip at her coffee, but thought it might place the kind of punctuation mark on her question sound dismissive. \'91What do you need?\'92 she added, softer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The keys to the kingdom?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Literal room keys, cabinet keys? Or passwords and auth codes?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Both, I suspect.\'92 Haley\'92s lips thinned as she contemplated the magnitude of what she was contemplating. Still, she forged a career as one of the few honest police officers in contemporary Ulysses when being corrupt was the easiest path to promotion. She\'92d investigated powerful people before, though none quite as powerful as Eloise.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Well, I don\'92t have any physical keys, but I know someone who does.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>I took Hayley a moment before her head cleared. \'91Bala Chuglingham.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92s facilities manager to pretty much the whole compound now. And he owes his current station in life to you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, and I won\'92t allow him to lose it on my account.\'92 As her words came out, Hayley thought they sounded almost whimsical.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took the moment to finally sip her coffee. \'91Are you telling me the great Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning doesn\'92t know how to protect a source?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If I use Bala\'92s pass keys, Eloise will be able to trace every movement I make, and how I made them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If a career in Security has taught me anything,\'92 Sarah countered, \'91it\'92s that, after a certain point, security is more about effort versus reward rather than absolute impenetrability.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley knew that to be true.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The archives smelled of ozone and old paper, an impossible combination that made Hayley's skin crawl. She'd made it past security with her Shakti badge and a confidence born from years of walking into places she didn't belong. The timer on her phone showed twelve minutes elapsed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jamison Hardy's files were exactly where they shouldn't be\'97misfiled under "Harmonics, Failed Experiments" instead of "Personnel Records." The deliberate obfuscation made Hayley's detective instincts sing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She photographed page after page, her phone's silent camera capturing decades of buried research. Hardy's early work on "multiversal frequency alignment" read like science fiction, except for the mathematical proofs that made her head spin. More interesting were the margin notes in different handwriting: *"Could destabilize local reality matrix"* and *"Consciousness transfer applications?"*\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A new folder caught her eye: "Project Precursor." Inside, schematics that looked hauntingly familiar. Not quite the Gyges Disc, not quite stasis technology, but something in between. An evolutionary link.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The lights flickered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley's phone showed eighteen minutes elapsed. Still safe, but the flickering lights suggested the Disc testing might be affecting building systems. She grabbed the Precursor folder, shoving it inside her jacket.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That's when she saw the signature on the authorization form: *E. Pallas, Project Director.* \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise had been involved from the beginning.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The lights went out completely.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Emergency lighting kicked in, bathing everything in hellish red. Hayley's phone buzzed: Sarah's warning code. Time to go.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She made it halfway to the door before the regular lights returned, revealing Eloise standing in the doorway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Ms. Chen," Eloise said, her voice carrying the weight of absolute authority. "I believe you're lost."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley straightened, hand still pressed against the folder inside her jacket. Every instinct screamed at her to run, but there was nowhere to go. "I was looking for historical resonance data. For my integration analysis."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"In the personnel archives?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I got turned around. This place is a maze."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise stepped into the room, her presence seeming to fill the space. "Do you know what I find fascinating about former law enforcement officers, Ms. Chen? They never quite lose the habit of investigation. Even when it puts them in considerable danger."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I don't know what you mean."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The folder inside your jacket suggests otherwise." Eloise moved closer, each step precise and deliberate. "Project Precursor. I haven't thought about that designation in years. Tell me, what did you hope to find?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley's mind raced through options. Denial was pointless. Aggression was suicide. That left truth, or something close to it. "Connections. Between the stasis technology and whatever you're building here."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"And did you find them?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Yes."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise smiled, a expression that didn't reach her eyes. "Good. Then you understand why your discretion is so important. Not just for your safety, but for Sarah's. For the child she carries. For the future we're all building, whether we know it or not."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Are you threatening them?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm protecting them. There's a difference." Eloise held out her hand. "The folder, please."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley pulled out the folder, but didn't hand it over. "I want guarantees. Sarah and her child stay safe. No experiments. No interference."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You're hardly in a position to negotiate."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm exactly in a position to negotiate. You didn't know I was here until I triggered something\'97probably a passive sensor in the Precursor files. That means your security has gaps. Gaps I've been mapping since I arrived." It was a bluff, mostly, but Hayley had learned that confidence sold more lies than evidence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise's smile widened fractionally. "You would have made an excellent addition to the original project team. Very well. Sarah and her child remain under my protection, free from any interference beyond standard medical monitoring. In exchange, you share everything you've discovered about the stasis technology, and you report directly to me about any future findings."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Agreed."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley handed over the folder. Eloise accepted it without looking at the contents.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"One more thing," Eloise said. "Your midnight investigations end now. If you want answers, you ask me directly. The next time I find you in restricted areas, our arrangement ends. Understood?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Understood."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Excellent. Now, I believe the Disc testing is about to conclude. You should return to your assigned duties." Eloise stepped aside, clearing the path to the door. "Oh, and Ms. Chen? Do give my regards to Kathy. It must be challenging, maintaining professional distance when you share such... intimate quarters."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley left without responding, but she felt Eloise's gaze burning into her back all the way to the elevator. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Twenty-eight minutes total. Sarah would be worried.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But Hayley had what she came for\'97not the folder, but the confirmation that Project Precursor connected everything. The stasis units, the Gyges Disc, Eloise's interest in Sarah's pregnancy. Pieces of a puzzle that was finally taking shape.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now she just had to figure out if they were building a better future, or racing toward catastrophe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The elevator doors closed, and Hayley allowed herself one moment of trembling before steel returned to her spine. She'd taken responsibility, protected Sarah, and gained Eloise's attention\'97for better or worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Time to find out which.
